.TH CP 1 2026-10-20 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
cp \- copy files and directories
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cp
.RI [ options ]
.I source dest
.br
.B cp
.RI [ options ]
.IR source ...\&
.I directory
.br
.B cp
.RI [ options ]
.BI "\-t " directory
.IR source ...\&
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cp
copies files, and with
.B \-R
directories, to another name or into a directory.
It is the
.BR busybox (1)
applet, which has fewer options than GNU
.BR cp ;
see
.BR NOTES .
.SS Operands
With
.BR \-t ,
every operand is a
.I source
and is copied into
.IR directory .
Otherwise, with two operands,
.I source
is copied to
.I dest
itself, except that when
.I dest
is an existing directory, or a symbolic link or junction to one,
.I source
is copied into it.
.B \-T
always copies to
.IR dest .
With more than two operands, the last is
.IR directory .
.PP
A copy into
.I directory
is named after the last component of
.IR source ,
with trailing slashes and backslashes removed, so
.B cp \-r src/ out
makes
.I out/src
when
.I out
exists.
.B cp
does not check beforehand that
.I directory
exists or is a directory: the copy of each
.I source
then fails on its own.
.PP
It is an error for a destination to be the same file as its source.
.B cp
compares the volume and file index, so names that differ only in case,
and hard links to one file, are detected.
.SS What is copied
A regular file is copied to a new file.
A directory is copied only with
.BR \-R :
it is created if it does not exist, or merged with the existing
directory if it does, and all its entries, hidden ones included, are
copied into it.
Without
.BR \-R ,
a directory is reported as omitted, and the exit status is 1.
.PP
A symbolic link is followed, and the file it points to is copied, unless
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-P ,
.BR \-R ,
or
.B \-a
is given and
.B \-L
is not.
Then the link itself is copied, as a new symbolic link with the same
target, except that
.B \-H
still follows a link named as an operand.
Junctions and app execution aliases are symbolic links for this purpose.
.PP
Without
.BR \-R ,
any other source that is not a directory is copied by reading it, so a
device or standard input can be a source.
With
.BR \-R ,
a source that is not a regular file, directory, or symbolic link, such
as a device, cannot be copied and is an error.
.SS Existing destinations
.B cp
never writes into an existing destination file.
It creates the destination as a new file, and when the name is taken it
deletes the old file, even a read-only one, and creates it again.
So other hard links to the old file keep its contents, a symbolic link
at the destination is replaced rather than followed, and the old file's
attributes are lost.
A file that cannot be deleted, such as a running program, is not
replaced, but loses any read-only attribute, and the copy fails with
.BR "File exists" .
.B \-n
and
.B \-u
skip some existing destinations, and
.B \-i
asks first.
.PP
A new file has the read-only attribute of its source and the current
time as its modification time; see
.BR \-p .
.SS Output
.B cp
reports each error on standard error as
.BI "cp: " message
and goes on with the remaining entries and operands.
Prompts go to standard error.
Only
.B \-v
writes to standard output.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-rv .
They may follow operands unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
The argument of
.B \-t
may be attached, as in
.BR \-tbin .
A long option may be abbreviated to any unambiguous prefix, and takes
its argument after
.B =
or as the next argument.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-archive
Same as
.BR \-dpR .
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-r ", " \-\-recursive
Copy directories recursively.
Implies
.BR \-d .
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-P ", " \-\-no\-dereference
Copy symbolic links as links, not the files they point to, including
links named as operands unless
.B \-H
is given.
Overridden by
.BR \-L ,
wherever it appears.
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\-dereference
Follow every symbolic link, including those found while recursing, and
copy the file it points to.
Overrides
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-P ,
and the
.B \-d
implied by
.B \-R
and
.BR \-a .
.TP
.B \-H
Follow symbolic links named as operands.
With
.BR \-R ,
links found while recursing are still copied as links.
Has no effect when links are followed anyway.
.TP
.B \-p
Preserve attributes.
Set the access and modification times of each copied file and directory
to the source's modification time, in whole seconds, and its read-only
attribute to the source's.
A directory gets its time after its contents are copied, and never
becomes read-only.
Ownership is not changed.
A failure to set these is reported but does not change the exit status.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Has no effect.
An existing destination is always replaced, as described above.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive
Before replacing an existing destination file, write
.BI "cp: overwrite \(aq" dest "\(aq?\&"
on standard error and read a line from standard input.
The destination is replaced only if the first character of the line
that is not a space or tab is
.B y
or
.BR Y ,
and otherwise it is skipped, which is not an error.
Existing directories are merged without asking.
The prompt appears whenever creating the destination fails, so it can
also appear for a destination that does not exist, and a
.B y
then reports the error.
.B \-i
and
.B \-n
override each other, and the last one given wins.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no\-clobber
Skip, without a message, each source whose destination exists.
This includes directories: with
.BR \-R ,
an existing destination directory is skipped whole, and nothing is
copied into it.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-link
Make hard links instead of copying files.
With
.BR \-R ,
directories are created and the files in them linked.
The file system must support hard links, and both names must be on the
same volume.
Cannot be combined with
.BR \-s .
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-symbolic\-link
Make symbolic links instead of copying files.
With
.BR \-R ,
directories are created and the files in them linked.
Each link's target is
.I source
as written, so a relative
.I source
is resolved relative to the directory containing the link, not to the
current directory.
Cannot be combined with
.BR \-l .
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-no\-target\-directory
Copy to
.I dest
itself, never into it.
With
.BR \-R ,
a source directory is copied to
.IR dest ,
and merged with it if it is an existing directory.
A source that is not a directory, and a
.I dest
that is, is an error.
Requires exactly two operands, and is ignored with
.BR \-t .
.TP
\fB\-t\fR \fIdirectory\fR, \fB\-\-target\-directory\fR=\fIdirectory\fR
Copy every operand into
.IR directory .
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-update
Skip a source that is not a directory when its destination exists and
is not older, comparing modification times in whole seconds.
Directories are always entered, so their contents are compared one by
one.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Write
.BI \(aq source "\(aq \-> \(aq" dest \(aq
on standard output for each file, directory, and symbolic link copied.
A directory is written after its contents.
Links made by
.B \-l
or
.BR \-s ,
skipped files, and copies of devices are not written.
.TP
.B \-\-remove\-destination
Has no effect.
An existing destination is always replaced, as described above.
.TP
.B \-\-parents
Copy each source to the path formed by appending it, as written, to
.IR directory ,
creating any missing directories on the way.
Leading slashes of
.I source
are dropped, and a drive letter makes the creation of the directories
fail.
When creating them fails,
.B cp
stops at once, without copying the remaining sources.
With two operands,
.I dest
must be an existing directory.
With more operands, or with
.BR \-t ,
.I directory
is created when it does not exist.
An existing destination file is deleted before its source is opened, so
it is lost if the source cannot be opened.
With
.BR \-v ,
.BI "removed \(aq" dest \(aq
is written for each one deleted.
.TP
.B \-\-help
As the first argument only, write a usage message on standard output
and exit with status 0.
The message omits the long options and
.BR \-v .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the arguments are expanded when busybox starts, for a caller
such as
.B cmd.exe
that does not expand them.
When it is unset, busybox sets it to
.B 0
for the programs it starts, so commands run from the kit's shell get the
shell's expansion only.
.TP
.B BB_UMASK
An octal file mode creation mask, applied before
.B cp
runs.
Only its owner write bit,
.BR 0200 ,
matters here: new files are then created read-only, unless
.B \-p
sets the attribute from the source.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, the first operand ends the options.
.PP
Messages written to a console also depend on
.BR BB_SKIP_ANSI_EMULATION ,
.BR BB_TERMINAL_MODE ,
.BR LANG ,
.BR LC_ALL ,
and
.BR LC_CTYPE ,
as for every busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including sources skipped by
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-u ,
or an answer to
.BR \-i .
.TP
.B 1
An invalid command line, with a usage message on standard error, or any
operand or directory entry that could not be copied.
.SH EXAMPLES
Keep a copy of a file before editing it, with its modification time:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cp \-p config.h config.h.orig
.EE
.RE
.PP
Copy the C sources into a directory, listing each copy:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cp \-v *.c backup
\(aqhello.c\(aq \-> \(aqbackup/hello.c\(aq
\(aqmain.c\(aq \-> \(aqbackup/main.c\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
Copy a directory tree with its times and symbolic links:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cp \-a src src.orig
.EE
.RE
.PP
Merge one directory into another, replacing only the files that are
newer in the source:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cp \-ruT assets build/assets
.EE
.RE
.PP
Copy only the files that do not exist in the destination:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cp \-n *.txt backup
.EE
.RE
.PP
Copy headers into a directory tree of their own, keeping their relative
paths:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cp \-\-parents \-t include src/*/*.h
.EE
.RE
.PP
Copy a program to a Windows path:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cp hello.exe c:/tools/
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR cp :
.IP \(bu 2
An existing destination file is deleted and created again, rather than
overwritten in place, even when it is read-only.
No prompt is shown unless
.B \-i
is given.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-f
and
.B \-\-remove\-destination
have no effect.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-n
skips an existing destination directory, instead of merging into it.
.IP \(bu 2
A destination directory that is missing, or is not a directory, is not
detected before copying starts.
.IP \(bu 2
Hard links among the sources are not kept, even with
.BR \-a :
each name is copied as a separate file.
.IP \(bu 2
Copying a directory into itself copies it once, then stops with
.B recursion detected
and exit status 1.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-p
sets the access time to the modification time, drops fractions of a
second, and does not change ownership.
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-v
writes a directory after its contents, and nothing for links made by
.B \-l
or
.BR \-s .
.IP \(bu 2
Options not listed above, such as
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-S ,
.BR \-x ,
.BR \-Z ,
.BR \-\-backup ,
.BR \-\-preserve ,
.BR \-\-no\-preserve ,
.BR \-\-reflink ,
.BR \-\-sparse ,
and
.BR \-\-strip\-trailing\-slashes ,
are invalid.
.PP
Apart from the times set by
.BR \-p ,
only a file's data and its read-only attribute are copied.
Alternate data streams, such as the
.B Zone.Identifier
stream that marks downloaded files, other attributes, such as hidden,
and security descriptors are not: a new file or directory gets the
permissions it inherits from its parent directory.
.PP
With
.BR \-R ,
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-P ,
or
.BR \-a ,
and without
.BR \-L ,
a symbolic link, junction, or app execution alias is copied as a
symbolic link to the same target, never as a junction or alias.
Creating a symbolic link, here or with
.BR \-s ,
needs Developer Mode or an elevated administrator, and otherwise fails
with
.BR "Permission denied" .
.PP
The Unix device names that busybox accepts, such as
.I /dev/null
and
.IR /dev/stdin ,
work as a destination, and as a source without
.BR \-R ;
see
.BR busybox (1).
With
.BR \-R ,
copying from a device fails with
.BR "Function not implemented" .
.I /dev/zero
and
.I /dev/urandom
have no end, and a copy of either runs until the disk is full.
.PP
Paths may have drive letters and backslashes.
Names in a directory are joined with
.BR / ,
so
.B cp \-v x \(aqc:\etools\(aq
writes names such as
.IR c:\etools/x .
A bare drive such as
.I c:
names the current directory of that drive, so
.B cp x c:
writes
.IR c:x ,
and
.B cp \-r c: dir
copies that directory to
.I dir
when
.I dir
does not exist.
Names may contain any Unicode character on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later, and are limited to the system code page on earlier versions.
A full path of 260 or more characters, or 248 or more for a directory
that
.B cp
creates, counting the current directory for a relative path, fails.
With
.BR \-R ,
a source directory that cannot be listed, such as one whose full path is
258 or 259 characters, is created empty with no error message, though
.B \-v
lists it, and the exit status is 1.
.PP
From
.BR cmd.exe ,
.B cp
expands wildcards in its arguments itself; see
.BR BB_GLOBBING .
Data are copied byte for byte, with no newline translation.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR install (1),
.BR ln (1),
.BR mv (1)
